<style>
  /* --- First-screen budget fixes (desktop hero re-budget) ---------------
     Two rules below fix/implement the desktop (>=1024px) first-screen
     layout. Both are scoped (Astro auto-attributes every selector here),
     so - like .app-tile et al. further down - they ship only on this page,
     never inlined into the other 22 via the shared Tailwind utility bundle.
     That matters here specifically: an earlier version of this fix used
     one-off `!`-prefixed Tailwind utilities (`!px-6`, `min-[1024px]:!flex-1`,
     etc.) for the same two rules, and check-css-duplication.js's single-page-
     utility ratchet caught it - those utilities are real bytes in the one
     inlined stylesheet every page ships, paid for by all 22 pages that never
     use them, for a layout only this page has. Scoped classes are the
     correct tool for a genuinely one-off rule; reach for them before adding
     another arbitrary-value utility that only this page will ever use. */

  /* header:not(.tool-hero):not(.not-found-header) in global.css is a bare,
     unlayered rule (specificity 0-2-1: element + two :not() args, each
     counted as a class). Unlayered CSS always wins over anything in
     @layer utilities regardless of specificity, so this page's own header
     - not .tool-hero - was permanently pinned to that rule's padding
     (4.5rem/1.5rem/2.25rem, i.e. 72px/24px/36px) at every viewport width.
     A second bare rule at >=768px (`header:not(.tool-hero) { padding:
     3.5rem 1.5rem 1.5rem }`) was clearly meant to correct this for
     wider screens, but has lower specificity (0-1-1, only one :not()), so
     it silently lost the cascade and never applied - not just on desktop,
     everywhere, always. This one rule alone was costing the home page's
     header 88px of dead vertical space at 1024px+ (72px top down to the
     8px min-[1024px]:pt-2 that was authored as a Tailwind class and never
     rendered, plus 36px bottom down to 12px), which was the single largest
     contributor to the whole first-screen budget overflow this file fixes.
     Astro's own scoped <style> output is itself unlayered (same implicit
     layer as global.css's bare rule), so beating it takes higher
     specificity, not `!important` - repeating the class once brings this
     selector (2 classes + Astro's own auto attribute = 0-3-1) above the
     bare rule's 0-2-1, regardless of source order between the two
     stylesheets. This does not touch global.css: that file is shared by
     every page (tool pages, content pages, licenses, 404), and its own
     header rule has the identical dead-code problem there too - a fix
     scoped to this page only can't reach it, and re-verifying every other
     page's own layout budget is a separate piece of work. */
  .hero-header.hero-header {
    padding-block: 3.5rem var(--space-6);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
  }

  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .hero-header.hero-header {
      padding-block: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
      padding-inline: 0;
    }
  }

  /* The dropzone/demo slot's wrapper. At 1024px+ it grows to absorb
     whatever's left of the first-screen budget between the header and the
     tool dock (main's own min-[1024px]:!flex-1 already makes <main> do this
     at the outer level; this repeats it one level down so the slack lands
     here specifically, not as a bare gap between this box and the dock),
     then centers its fixed-height child inside that grown box. No
     specificity fight needed - .merge-tool's own bare rule (global.css,
     >=768px) only sets width/flex-shrink, and forcing flex-shrink back to
     0 here is what we want anyway.

     Still 1024px, unlike the two-column block below: this and the
     min-[1024px]: utilities in the markup are the *first-screen budget*
     (hero, dropzone and tool dock composed into one screen with the dock
     pinned to the bottom), which is worth having on any laptop. Only the
     side-by-side demo needs the wider window. */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .first-screen-frame {
      display: flex;
      flex: 1 1 auto;
      flex-direction: column;
      justify-content: center;
    }
  }

  /* --- The two-column first screen (DEMO-05 variant T) ------------------
     1320px, not the 1024px this started at. The left column has a hard
     940px floor (see the grid comment below) and the second track's flex
     factor is 0.55, so below about 1300px the demo column is whatever
     narrow strip is left over: measured at 132px on a 1180px iPad in
     landscape and 187px at 1280px, against a mockup that wants 300px plus
     its stage padding. The result was a postage-stamp phone with six-line
     caption wrapping beside a hero whose tool dock had wrapped to two rows
     anyway - so the 940px floor was not even buying what it exists for at
     those widths.

     Everything from 1320px up is unchanged; everything below now gets the
     stacked layout, which is the one this page had before the demo landed
     and which HeroDemo has been retuned for (shorter tracks, a larger
     mockup on tablets, panels that fade in rather than slide). That file
     switches on the same 1320px figure and the two must stay in step: a
     media query cannot read a custom property, so the number is duplicated
     and these comments are the only link between them.

     Two things here are load-bearing and both fail silently if changed.

     align-items: start. Grid items stretch to the row height by default,
     which for this row is the demo's ten screens, and a sticky element that
     is already as tall as its scroll range never moves. It has to be given
     its own height and allowed to be shorter than the row.

     No overflow, transform, filter or contain anywhere on .home-first or
     either column. Each of those creates a containing block that kills
     position: sticky on every descendant, with no error anywhere - and this
     subtree has three sticky layers in it: the left column, the demo's two
     panels, and the app bar above. */
  @media (min-width: 1320px) {
    .home-first {
      display: grid;
      /* The left column must stay wide enough for the tool dock to keep
         all nine tiles on one row. Nine 80px tiles with eight 20px gaps is
         880px before padding, and at 1.05fr the column came out at 794px,
         wrapped the dock to two rows, and pushed its bottom edge to 910 on
         an 800px viewport - the exact failure the whole layout exists to
         avoid. Measured, not guessed.

         The demo column carries a 340px floor of its own (300px mockup plus
         its stage's 20px padding either side) rather than minmax(0, ...).
         Without it the column is pure leftover, and because the two flex
         factors sum to 2 the leftover is not even distributed in full - at
         1280px the browser handed the column 187px and left 153px of the
         row simply unused. The floor makes the failure loud (the row
         overflows) instead of quiet (the demo shrinks to nothing), and the
         1320px breakpoint above keeps it from ever being reached. */
      grid-template-columns: minmax(940px, 1.45fr) minmax(340px, 0.55fr);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .home-first-main {
      position: sticky;
      top: 3.5rem;
      height: calc(100svh - 3.5rem);
    }

    /* The demo's panels size themselves from this, rather than from 100svh,
       so they fit the column between the app bar and the viewport bottom.
       See HeroDemo.module.css's --herodemo-panel-height comment: it is the
       documented hook for exactly this, and it inherits, so no prop
       plumbing is needed. */
    .home-first-demo {
      --herodemo-panel-height: calc(100svh - 3.5rem);
    }

    /* The demo ships its own title card, which earns its place when the demo
       is a section of its own further down the page. Beside the hero it is
       saying the same thing twice, in the same eyeline, and it costs the
       visitor a whole screen of scrolling before the phone appears at all.
       Hiding the whole track (not just the card inside it) is what makes it
       cost no scroll distance here. :global because it belongs to HeroDemo's
       CSS module and Astro's scoped styles do not reach into a child
       component. */
    .home-first-demo :global([data-herodemo-intro]) {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* --- The story-card stack ------------------------------------------
     Each card pins below the app bar and the next one slides up over it.
     Three things make that work and each is doing real work:

     - The cards are opaque. Both FeatureCard variants paint a solid surface
       or a solid gradient, so a covering card genuinely hides the one
       beneath. If a translucent variant is ever added, it cannot go here.
     - Paint order is DOM order. These are positioned elements with
       `z-index: auto`, so a later card paints over an earlier one without
       anybody managing a z-index scale. The app bar keeps its own z-20 and
       so still passes over all of them.
     - `position`, `min-height` and `top` all beat the utilities the cards
       carry. Astro's scoped output is unlayered and Tailwind's utilities are
       in @layer utilities, so these win regardless of specificity; sticky is
       still a positioned box, so CardDecor's absolute children go on
       anchoring to it exactly as before.

     768px because that is where FeatureCard's `density="screen"` gives each
     card its viewport-height floor. Below it the cards are content-height
     and run well past the viewport, and pinning a card taller than the
     screen just hides its own bottom.

     --stack-gap is the one number here: the air above a pinned card, the
     air below it, and the run-out after the last one. The card's own height
     is derived from it rather than left to the `min-h-[calc(100vh-6rem)]`
     utility, because that figure was chosen for cards that scroll past and
     it puts a pinned card flush against the app bar with 40px of dead space
     underneath - which is what a stacked card's margins should never be,
     since the top edge is the one the eye tracks.

     The shadow is a single upward cast. That is the edge that arrives over
     the card below, and it is the only edge that needs one: a downward
     shadow lands on the card that is still approaching from underneath and
     reads as a halo around the deck rather than as depth between cards. */
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .card-stack {
      /* The one number: the air between the app bar and a pinned card. */
      --stack-gap: 2rem;
    }

    .card-stack :global(.card-reveal) {
      position: sticky;
      top: calc(3.5rem + var(--stack-gap));
      /* Down to the bottom edge of the viewport, not to a matching gap
         above it, and that is a correctness rule rather than a taste one.

         These cards are content-height with a floor, so they are only equal
         while every one of them clears the floor - and the first card's copy
         does not, by a wide margin, on an ordinary laptop: measured natural
         heights are 739px at 1280px wide against a 740px slot on a 1512x860
         screen. One pixel. Any card that overruns pokes out below the
         shorter ones stacked over it, and since the tallest card is also the
         first, every later card would leave a band of it showing underneath.
         A card that runs to the bottom edge cannot: whatever is behind it is
         covered all the way down, however tall that is.

         The alternative was a media query gating the stack on a viewport
         tall enough for the tallest card, which is three measured
         breakpoints that go stale the next time anyone edits a paragraph. */
      min-height: calc(100svh - 3.5rem - var(--stack-gap));
      /* A single upward cast. That is the edge that arrives over the card
         below, and the only edge that needs one: a downward shadow lands on
         the card still approaching from underneath and reads as a halo
         around the deck rather than as depth between cards. */
      box-shadow: 0 -20px 46px -26px var(--color-primary-ring);
    }

    /* The footer is inside .card-stack, and that is what makes the ending
       work. It is the deck's own scroll run-out, so the last card gets a
       sticky range instead of the zero it would have if the wrapper ended at
       its bottom edge - and, because the wrapper now reaches the end of the
       document, the cards are never pushed up off their pinned position at
       all. Before this the deck rose by more than a hundred pixels over the
       last stretch, tucking the top card under the app bar and leaving a
       dead band between it and the footer.

       So nothing moves at the end: the footer simply slides up over the
       deck's bottom edge and docks there, arriving the same way every card
       did. :last-of-type rather than :last-child, since the footer is now
       the last child; the cards are the only <section>s in here. */
    .card-stack :global(section.card-reveal:last-of-type) {
      margin-block-end: 0;
    }

    /* The scroll-reveal fade and the stack are two answers to the same
       question, and here the stack wins. A card fades in from opacity 0
       while it is already overlapping the card it is covering, so for the
       length of the fade you see straight through it to the one behind -
       the one thing a stack must never do. Neutralised in CSS rather than
       by teaching FeatureCard about this page.

       `animation-name`, not the `animation` shorthand, which would also
       reset animation-timeline and animation-range out from under the rule
       it is overriding - harmless while the only intent is "stop the fade",
       and quietly wrong the moment anyone gives these cards a second
       animation. (The card's own `view-timeline-name` below is a different
       property and survives either way, so the decor parallax is not what
       is at stake here.) */
    .card-stack :global(.card-reveal) {
      animation-name: none;
    }
  }

  /* The tool grid's tiles. Written as utilities on the element this was ~1.6KB
     of class attribute per tile, but the real cost was where it landed: around
     thirty arbitrary-value rules (two gradients, three layered shadows, a
     per-state transition, the hover/focus/active transform set) in Tailwind's
     utility layer, which is one site-wide bundle inlined whole into all twelve
     pages. Every tool page was carrying the home page's hover animation. This
     is one component's own decoration, used nowhere else and composed of
     nothing reusable, so it belongs beside .app-tooltip below - already here
     for the same reason - where Astro scopes it and ships it on this page only.

     The transition is also a fix. `transition-[transform_0.4s_var(--ease-spring),
     ...]` sets transition-*property*, so it compiled to `transition-property:
     transform .4s var(--ease-spring), ...` - not a list of property names, so
     the whole declaration was invalid and the tiles snapped between states with
     no animation at all. Same for the active state's. Restored here as what it
     was plainly meant to be. Note Tailwind v4 animates transforms through the
     individual `translate`/`rotate`/`scale` properties rather than `transform`,
     which is why those are named here instead. */
  .app-tile {
    border-radius: 18px;
    background: linear-gradient(150deg, var(--color-muted-light), var(--color-border-strong));
    color: var(--color-surface);
    box-shadow: 0 8px 18px -8px var(--color-primary-ring), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5);
    transition: translate 0.4s var(--ease-spring), rotate 0.4s var(--ease-spring), scale 0.4s var(--ease-spring),
      box-shadow 0.4s var(--ease-out), background-color 0.2s var(--ease-out);
  }

  /* Accented tiles carry their own gradient; hover overrides both, which is why
     these rules come first and the state rules after.

     Keyed off the tool's own `accent` field (src/data/tools.js), NOT off
     :first-child/:last-child as this once was. The positional version pointed
     the highlight at whatever tool happened to sit last in that array, so
     reordering the array by SEO priority silently moved the accent onto an
     unrelated tool. :last-child was the wrong hook regardless: the grid wraps,
     so the last tile is only a visual corner at some widths. */
  .app-card[data-accent='primary'] .app-tile {
    background: linear-gradient(150deg, var(--color-primary-hover), var(--color-primary));
  }

  .app-card[data-accent='soft'] .app-tile {
    background: linear-gradient(150deg, var(--color-surface), var(--color-success-soft));
    color: var(--color-success);
  }

  /* :global because the icon is a component's output, not this template's own
     markup, so Astro's scope attribute never reaches it. */
  .app-tile :global(svg) {
    display: block;
    width: 55%;
    height: 55%;
    transition: rotate 0.4s var(--ease-spring), scale 0.4s var(--ease-spring);
  }

  .app-card:hover .app-tile,
  .app-card:focus-visible .app-tile {
    translate: 0 -3px;
    rotate: 1deg;
    scale: 1.04;
    background: var(--color-primary);
    color: var(--color-surface);
    box-shadow: 0 12px 20px -8px var(--color-primary-ring), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5);
  }

  .app-card:hover .app-tile :global(svg),
  .app-card:focus-visible .app-tile :global(svg) {
    rotate: -1deg;
    scale: 1.08;
  }

  .app-card:active .app-tile {
    translate: 0 0;
    scale: 0.95;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 10px -4px var(--color-primary-ring);
    transition: translate 0.1s ease, scale 0.1s ease, box-shadow 0.1s ease;
  }

  .app-tooltip {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: calc(100% + 14px);
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%) translateY(4px) scale(0.95);
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    background: var(--color-surface);
    color: var(--color-text);
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    border-radius: 16px;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    width: max-content;
    max-width: 200px;
    text-align: center;
    transition: opacity 0.2s ease, visibility 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease;
    z-index: 10;
    pointer-events: none;
    line-height: 1.4;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-md);
  }

  .app-tooltip::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 100%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    border-width: 6px;
    border-style: solid;
    border-color: var(--color-surface) transparent transparent transparent;
  }

  .app-card:hover .app-tooltip,
  .app-card:focus-visible .app-tooltip {
    opacity: 1;
    visibility: visible;
    transform: translateX(-50%) translateY(0) scale(1);
    transition: opacity 0.5s ease-out 0.4s, visibility 0.5s ease-out 0.4s, transform 0.5s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1) 0.4s;
  }

  /* --- Card parallax --------------------------------------------------
     Depth on the story cards below the first screen: each card's
     background sketch and its watermark icon drift vertically against the
     card's own content as the card crosses the viewport, the icon further
     (larger travel) than the sketch. Nothing in the card's text moves, so
     this reads as layers behind the content rather than as the content
     itself sliding.

     Driven entirely by CSS scroll-driven animations, so there is no scroll
     listener, no rAF loop and no new script - and therefore no new inline
     script for the CSP hash list to cover. The timeline is published by the
     card (view-timeline-name) and consumed by its own descendants; timeline
     names resolve up the ancestor chain, so five cards can share one name
     and each decor layer still finds its own card's timeline. The name has
     to live on the card rather than on the decor itself: the cards are
     `overflow-hidden`, which makes each one a scroll container, and a
     `view()` timeline declared inside would measure against that container -
     which never scrolls - instead of against the page.

     Two guards, both load-bearing:

     - @supports. Without scroll-driven animation support `animation-
       duration: auto` is invalid, so the declaration is dropped, the
       duration falls back to 0s, and `fill: both` would freeze every decor
       layer at the *end* of its keyframes - a permanent 56px offset rather
       than no effect. The feature query is what makes the unsupported path
       "no animation" instead of "wrong position".
     - prefers-reduced-motion. Scroll-linked movement is exactly the kind of
       motion the preference exists to suppress, and the card-reveal fade in
       FeatureCard.astro sits behind the same pair of guards.

     `transform`, not the `translate` property, on purpose: Tailwind v4
     compiles -translate-y-1/2 (which CardDecor's `cr`/`cl` icon positions
     use for vertical centering) to `translate`, so animating that property
     would silently clobber the centering on two of the five cards. */
  @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      :global(.card-reveal) {
        view-timeline-name: --feature-card;
      }

      :global(.decor-parallax),
      :global(.decor-parallax-icon) {
        animation-duration: auto;
        animation-timing-function: linear;
        animation-fill-mode: both;
        animation-timeline: --feature-card;
      }

      :global(.decor-parallax) {
        animation-name: decor-drift;
      }

      :global(.decor-parallax-icon) {
        animation-name: decor-drift-icon;
      }

      /* From negative to positive over the card's whole cover range: as the
         card travels up the screen the layer lags behind it, which is what
         "further away" looks like, and the icon lags further than the sketch
         so the two read as separate depths.

         160px and 240px of total travel, which is a lot for a background -
         and it needs to be, because from 768px up the card itself pins
         while this runs (see .card-stack below). A stationary card with a
         moving background is where the whole effect actually reads; at the
         amplitudes that felt right on a scrolling card it was nearly
         invisible on a pinned one.

         The sketch's amplitude stays under the 110px of vertical inflation
         CardDecor gives that box, so the drift never exposes an uncovered
         strip. The watermark icon needs no such margin: it already overhangs
         the card on every side and is clipped by it. */
      @keyframes decor-drift {
        from {
          transform: translateY(-80px);
        }
        to {
          transform: translateY(80px);
        }
      }

      @keyframes decor-drift-icon {
        from {
          transform: translateY(-120px);
        }
        to {
          transform: translateY(120px);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  @keyframes pulse {
    0% {
      transform: scale(0.95);
      box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-success) 70%, transparent);
    }
    70% {
      transform: scale(1);
      box-shadow: 0 0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-success) 0%, transparent);
    }
    100% {
      transform: scale(0.95);
      box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-success) 0%, transparent);
    }
  }

</style>
